What stock clients can actually send — the constraint that picks the design
2026-08-10 · grafana/grafana:latest, telegraf:1.39.2
================================================================================

Data-plane auth breaks every existing client, so the mechanism is not a
free choice: it has to be something Grafana and Telegraf can already be
configured to send. Rather than assume, both were pointed at recording
servers (a pyarrow Flight server and an HTTP handler that log headers
and refuse) and driven through every auth configuration they offer.

GRAFANA — InfluxDB datasource, SQL/Flight SQL mode
  Configuration                     What reached the server
  --------------------------------  ------------------------------------
  secureJsonData.token              authorization: Bearer <token>   YES
  basicAuth + basicAuthPassword     (nothing)                       NO
  httpHeaderName1/httpHeaderValue1  (nothing)                       NO

  Only the `database` header and gRPC's own (:authority, te, user-agent,
  grpc-*) accompany the call otherwise.

  THIS IS THE WHOLE CONSTRAINT. Grafana's basic-auth toggle and its
  custom-header escape hatch are HTTP-only — they are not forwarded onto
  the gRPC path, so neither can carry a credential to Flight SQL. Any
  design resting on basic auth or a bespoke header (X-TimeLake-Token,
  say) would work for curl and fail for Grafana, which is the one client
  FR-8 makes first-class. The token field is the only door.

TELEGRAF — the write half
  Plugin                Endpoint          Header
  --------------------  ----------------  ----------------------------
  outputs.influxdb_v2   /api/v2/write     Authorization: Token <token>
  outputs.influxdb      /write, /query    Authorization: Basic b64(u:p)

DESIGN FORCED BY THE ABOVE
  One credential — a token — accepted under three spellings on one
  header, because three stock clients spell the same thing differently:

    Authorization: Bearer <token>   Grafana (Flight SQL), Tributary
    Authorization: Token  <token>   Telegraf influxdb_v2
    Authorization: Basic  <b64>     Telegraf influxdb v1, password=token

  This is also what InfluxDB itself accepts, so the compatibility matrix
  in the reference stays true rather than gaining an exception.

  Rejected on this evidence: HTTP basic auth as the primary mechanism
  (invisible to Grafana over Flight), and a custom header such as
  X-TimeLake-Token (same problem, plus it would need a Grafana plugin
  fork — FR-8 says stock).

RIG
  bench/compose/tls-drill/auth_probe.py   Flight SQL metadata recorder
  bench/compose/tls-drill/http_probe.py   HTTP Authorization recorder
